course: dictionary
lesson: 14187
title: Morally: right and wrong
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Morally is an adverb. It tells us about right and wrong behaviour, which means how a person should act to be good and fair. «Is it morally right to tell a white lie?» The words right and wrong are the clues: this sentence asks about good and bad behaviour.
explain: Something can be legal and still be morally wrong. «It was legal to take the last cake, but it was morally wrong, because his sister had asked for it.» Legal means allowed by the law. Morally asks a different question: is it good and fair?
explain: When you meet morally, try swapping in "in a way that is about right and wrong". If the sentence still makes sense, you have the meaning. «Sam felt morally bound to tell the teacher.» Morally bound means Sam felt it was his duty to do it.
fact: Morally means in a way that is about right and wrong behaviour.
fact: «Morally right» means right by our sense of what is good and fair.
fact: «Morally wrong» means wrong by our sense of what is good and fair, even if it is not against the law.
fact: «Morally bound» or «morally obliged» means you feel it is your duty to do something.
fact: Morally can also mean in a good, honest way, as in «She always acted morally.»
exercise: «Is it morally right to tell a white lie?» What does morally mean in this sentence? | In a loud voice | Very quickly | About the weather | *About right and wrong behaviour
exercise: «Sam felt morally bound to tell the teacher who broke the window.» What does morally bound mean? | Tied up with rope | Happy and excited | *Feeling it is your duty to do something | Too tired to move
exercise: Ben says, «It was legal to take the ticket, but it was morally wrong.» Which word tells us the question is about right and wrong, not the law? | legal | *morally | took | ticket
test: «She acted morally, always honest and fair with her team.» How did she act? | In a hurry | *In a good, honest way | In a noisy way | In a messy way
test: Something can be legal but still ___ wrong. Which word fits best? | loudly | quickly | barely | *morally
exam: «Is it morally right to share your sweets with a friend who has none?» What does morally mean here? | *About right and wrong behaviour | About being very fast | About being very loud | About the weather
